---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="The design language">
  <p class="demo-note">
    This framework is a design philosophy for enterprise software first and a
    stylesheet second. The philosophy fits in one question — not "how can this
    look better?" but <strong>"what can I remove so the essential thing becomes
    obvious?"</strong> — and six principles below turn that question into rules
    a screen either follows or doesn't. Components emerge from the principles,
    never the other way around.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Design the decision, not the screen</h2>
    <p>Every screen exists to serve <strong>one decision its user must make</strong>.
    "The payroll manager needs to know whether payroll is safe to release" is a
    design brief; "we need a payroll screen" is not. The system may run fifty
    validations — the user reads <em>Ready · 428 employees · 2 exceptions</em>.
    Complexity lives under the interface, never in the user's mental model.</p>
    <p>Every screen must answer, in order: <strong>what is this → what should I
    look at → what should I do</strong>. And a screen is one step of a journey
    (request → validation → exception → resolution → approval → execution →
    confirmation) — a beautiful screen inside a terrible workflow is still a
    bad product, so each screen's exit lands the user at the next decision with
    their context intact. The <a href={base + '/patterns/invoice-list'}>pattern
    pages</a> are built this way: each opens with who uses it and what "done"
    looks like, before any markup.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The six rules</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Area</th>
          <th scope="col">Principle</th>
          <th scope="col">Where the framework enforces it</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Hierarchy</th>
            <td>One primary action per screen; secondary actions subordinate;
            destructive actions require explicit intent.</td>
            <td>One plain <a href={base + '/components/button'}><code>bo-btn</code></a>
            per screen — everything else takes <code>--secondary</code>,
            <code>--ghost</code>, or <code>--danger-ghost</code>. Measured, not
            aspirational: 18 of 19 pattern screens hold to ≤1 visually primary
            action (the wizard's Next/Submit pair is the exception, and even
            those are never both visible).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Typography</th>
            <td>Large = meaning, medium = context, small = supporting
            information. Size IS the hierarchy.</td>
            <td>The <a href={base + '/concepts/tokens'}>type-scale tokens</a> —
            components consume the scale, never free-pick sizes; density remaps
            one tier and every component follows.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Colour</th>
            <td>Neutral by default. Colour communicates state — never
            decoration.</td>
            <td>Semantic tones only (<code>danger/warning/success</code>), each
            AA-contrast-gated in both themes at build time, and every colour
            signal is two-channel: a visible non-colour cue plus a programmatic
            one, so meaning never rides on hue alone. See
            <a href={base + '/concepts/accessibility'}>the accessibility
            model</a> and <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>conditional
            cell tone</a> — the tone repeats a meaning already present in text.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Spacing</th>
            <td>Consistent rhythm. Whitespace separates concepts; proximity
            groups related things.</td>
            <td>The spacing scale in <a href={base + '/concepts/tokens'}>design
            tokens</a> plus layout primitives (<code>bo-cluster</code>,
            <code>bo-stack</code>, <code>bo-grid</code>) that carry the rhythm —
            pages compose primitives instead of hand-tuning margins.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Interaction</th>
            <td>Direct manipulation; avoid needless dialogs; preserve context;
            immediate feedback.</td>
            <td>Inline row editing over modal forms
            (<a href={base + '/components/inline-editing'}>data-row-edit</a>), errors
            that re-render in place with the user's work intact
            (<a href={base + '/concepts/concurrency'}>concurrency &amp;
            conflicts</a>), and focus restored across swaps so a keyboard user
            is never dumped on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Content</th>
            <td>Human language. Explain the consequence, not the mechanism.</td>
            <td><code>Processing Status: 04</code> is refused; "Ready to
            release" with detail underneath is the shape. Status strings name
            meanings, technology stays invisible — the user experiences "I
            clicked and the page responded," not an HTMX partial update. See
            <a href={base + '/getting-started/htmx'}>the HTMX guide</a>: markup
            carries structure, the server carries intelligence.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The field matrix — which design, per field type and context</h2>
    <p>Reusability decides what ships; <strong>suitability decides what a
    screen uses</strong>. When they conflict, the suitable design wins — an
    unsuitable control damages the UX no matter how reusable it is — and the
    resolution is always a suitable <em>reusable</em> primitive from this
    matrix, never a one-off. Contexts follow the
    <a href={base + '/components/inline-editing'}>editing-design chooser</a>:
    entry (bordered), read-mostly correction (seamless + row Save/Cancel),
    and display.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Field type</th>
          <th scope="col">Standalone form (entry)</th>
          <th scope="col">Read-mostly record/table</th>
          <th scope="col">Editable table (entry grid)</th>
          <th scope="col">Display</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Date</th>
            <td>Native <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code> in a form field — nothing to add</td>
            <td>Seamless <code>type="date"</code> cell</td>
            <td>Bordered <code>type="date"</code> cell</td>
            <td>Text; status (overdue) as a worded badge — <a href={base + '/components/amount'}>the date contract</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Number / rate / %</th>
            <td><code>.bo-input--numeric</code></td>
            <td>Seamless numeric</td>
            <td>Bordered numeric</td>
            <td><a href={base + '/components/amount'}>Amount</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Money</th>
            <td>Currency selectable → <a href={base + '/components/money'}>Money</a> ( currency | amount ); fixed → numeric, currency in the label</td>
            <td>Seamless numeric; the fixed currency shown as adjacent text</td>
            <td>Currency is its own column (fixed per row) → bordered numeric; currency changeable inline (rare) → the joined Money control in the cell</td>
            <td>Amount with <code>__currency</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Quantity</th>
            <td>Joined ( qty | unit ) — <a href={base + '/components/quantity'}>Quantity</a>; touch/warehouse adds the steppers</td>
            <td>Seamless input; unit as text</td>
            <td>Unit is its own column → bordered input; unit changeable inline (rare) → the joined control. Steppers never — they belong to touch, not dense grids</td>
            <td>Amount with <code>__unit</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Two axes generate every cell: the screen's
    job picks the chrome (bordered / seamless / plain text), and the
    qualifier's mutability picks joined-versus-split (changeable next to
    the value → joined control; fixed → label, adjacent text, or its own
    column). Nothing in the matrix is a new component — every cell is a
    setting of the four primitives.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Subtraction is the method</h2>
    <p>For every element — button, field, colour, screen, feature — the test
    is: <em>if I remove this, does the user's ability to accomplish the task
    materially decrease?</em> If no, it goes. Then ask again: 20 things → 12 →
    7 → 4 — then ask whether 4 is enough. Simplicity is not minimalism:
    minimalism is "there aren't many things," simplicity is <strong>"I
    understand what is happening."</strong> A dense ERP grid can be simple; a
    sparse screen can be confusing.</p>
    <p>This test is applied to the framework itself, not just to screens built
    with it. Additions are refused when they serve exactly one scenario or add
    a second way to do something that already works — and the refusals are
    recorded with reasons, the same as the things that shipped. If something
    needs a long explanation to justify its existence, that is the signal it
    should not exist.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Details are the design</h2>
    <p>Simplicity takes more effort, not less. Loading, empty, and error
    states are designed surfaces, not afterthoughts
    (<a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>state patterns</a>); every
    pattern page documents them in a states table because screens spend most
    of their life there. Keyboard behaviour, focus order, wording, and print
    output get the same treatment as the happy path — good design feels
    inevitable, not impressive.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Scoring alignment — one number per component</h2>
    <p>Every component carries a <strong>Design-System Alignment score</strong>:
    each of the six rules above, plus context-fit against the field matrix,
    scored 0-3 with a checkable citation (a build gate, a grep, a measured
    check — never an opinion). Rules that don't apply to a component leave
    the denominator instead of penalizing it, and the result reads as a
    percentage: how on-direction this component's design is. The score
    decides where alignment work goes — it is deliberately a different
    question from whether a component is <em>worth its surface</em>, which
    a separate keep/improve/deprecate review answers. Scores live with the
    review records and are re-taken when a component's design changes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The shipping filter</h2>
    <p>Before a screen ships, it faces ten questions — is the purpose statable
    in one sentence, is the primary action obvious, can anything be removed,
    does every element have a purpose, does it explain itself, is complexity
    hidden, is there decoration, are states understandable, does interaction
    preserve context, and would it look like this built from zero today. A
    "no" carries a consequence (remove, reword, simplify, redesign) — not a
    note. Every pattern in this gallery has been through it, and the deeper
    principle behind all ten:</p>
    <p><strong>Design is the process of deciding what deserves to exist.</strong>
    Don't design screens — design decisions. Don't expose processes — expose
    outcomes. Don't add functionality — remove friction. Don't make complex
    systems look simple — make them feel simple.</p>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/concepts/tokens', 'Design tokens'],
    ['/concepts/accessibility', 'Accessibility model'],
    ['/concepts/density', 'Density'],
    ['/patterns/invoice-list', 'Invoice-list pattern'],
    ['/components/state-patterns', 'State patterns'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
